===Alternative forms===
* [[trepeget]] {{qualifier|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|trebuchet|lang=fro}}, {{term|trebuket}} et al. (modern {{term|trébuchet|lang=fr}}), from {{term|trebuchier||to overthrow, topple|lang=fro}}, from {{term|tre-|lang=fro}} + {{m|fro|*buchier}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|buc||trunk of the body|lang=fro}}, from Old {{etyl|frk|en}} {{m|frk|*buk|*būk|belly, trunk, torso}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*būkaz||belly, abdomen, trunk}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*bʰōw-||to blow, swell}}. Cognate with {{etyl|goh|-}} {{m|goh|būh||belly}}, {{etyl|ang|-}} {{m|ang|būc||belly, trunk}}. More at {{l|en|bouk}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈtɹɛbəʃɛt/|/ˈtɹɛb.jə.ʃeɪ/|/ˈtɹɛb.ju.ʃeɪ/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{enPR|trěb’yo͞o-shet}}, {{IPA|/ˈtɹɛb.juˌʃɛt/|/ˈtɹɛb.jəˌʃeɪ/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A medieval [[siege engine]] consisting of a large pivoting arm heavily weighted on one end. Considered to be the technological successor to the [[catapult]].
# A [[torture]] device for [[dunk]]ing suspected [[witch]]es by means of a [[chair]] attached to the end of a long pole.

